From: hawkff <109485367+hawkff@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 23:01:31 +0000
Subject: Add an executable memory rule to the Android seccomp policy

BaselinePolicyAndroid gets an option that governs mmap, mmap2, mprotect
and pkey_mprotect calls that ask for PROT_EXEC. By default nothing
changes.

In the deny mode such a call crashes the process with the usual seccomp
crash report, as the Android GPU process policy already does for every
PROT_EXEC request. The rule refuses new requests for executable memory
through mmap, mmap2, mprotect and pkey_mprotect. It looks only at the
prot argument, because a filter cannot see what a file descriptor refers
to, so libraries cannot be loaded after the filter starts either. Memory
that is already mapped both writable and executable stays usable, which
includes the code cache of the Java runtime in renderers on Android
versions before 17, and the rule does not cover writes through
/proc/self/mem.

In the report mode such a call is logged under the Android log tag
cr_seccomp, followed by the /proc/self/maps line of the affected memory,
and then performed, so that a test run can list every executable mapping
a process creates. The handler performs mprotect and pkey_mprotect again
with a marker in an argument that the kernel ignores, which the filter
lets through. It performs mmap and mmap2 in two steps: first without
PROT_EXEC, then it adds PROT_EXEC with such an mprotect. If the second
step fails, the handler logs that and ends the process as the deny mode
does, because the first step may already have replaced memory at a fixed
address. The report mode protects nothing.

